9
00:00:22.599 --> 00:00:25.137
The grocery platform's rate-limiting service.

10
00:00:25.417 --> 00:00:36.233
It sets per-customer limits at the gateway, enforces partner API contracts, protects OTP against SMS-pumping fraud, and paces calls to outbound vendors.

11
00:00:36.513 --> 00:00:39.948
Ch 18 compared the algorithms on one client.

12
00:00:40.228 --> 00:00:48.185
This chapter designs the system: millions of keys, many gateway nodes, two regions, adversaries, and the limiter's own failures.

13
00:00:49.535 --> 00:00:51.818
L1: state per key (real Redis).

14
00:00:52.098 --> 00:01:01.869
An exact sliding-window log for 100,000 users took 987 bytes per key, ~9.9 GB at 10M keys.

15
00:01:02.149 --> 00:01:09.914
GCRA took 90 bytes, one integer, and made exactly the token bucket's decisions (0 of 2.2M differ).

16
00:01:10.194 --> 00:01:23.869
On bursty traffic the sliding-window counter disagreed with the exact log on 4.3% of decisions and let a key reach 108 requests in a true 60-second window against a limit of 60.

17
00:01:25.219 --> 00:01:28.824
L2: one global limit from 8 nodes (real Redis Lua).

18
00:01:29.104 --> 00:01:35.696
A partner with a 1,000 req per second contract sent 900 req per second, skewed onto one node.

19
00:01:35.976 --> 00:01:40.840
Local per-node limits rejected 4,093 in-contract requests.

20
00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:45.301
A central GCRA rejected 0, at 1 Redis call per request.

21
00:01:45.581 --> 00:01:54.968
Adaptive token leasing rejected 0 with 0.17 calls per request, and still held the cap at 2,000 req per second offered.

22
00:01:56.318 --> 00:01:57.171
L3:

23
00:01:57.451 --> 00:01:59.201
OTP SMS pumping.

24
00:01:59.481 --> 00:02:11.043
Per-IP limits sent 47,175 attack SMS and locked out 57.5% of real users, who share carrier CGNAT addresses.

25
00:02:11.323 --> 00:02:18.299
Per-phone limits blocked no real users but let 59,199 attack SMS through.

26
00:02:18.579 --> 00:02:33.449
Per-destination-range budgets plus attestation cut the attack to 79 SMS (356 rupeesinstead of 266,396 rupees), with 3 real users affected.

27
00:02:34.799 --> 00:02:36.761
L4: regions and failures.

28
00:02:37.041 --> 00:02:46.791
A static 500 or 500 split between regions refused 99,370 in-contract requests when the partner's traffic moved.

29
00:02:47.071 --> 00:02:50.527
Rebalancing every 10 s refused 914.

30
00:02:50.807 --> 00:02:58.188
A central limiter admitted 1,468 per second against a 1,000 contract during a partition.

31
00:02:58.468 --> 00:03:18.137
With the limiter's Redis down for 2 minutes, failing open sent 2,059 attack SMS (9,265 rupees), failing closed refused 3,955 real logins, and a local fallback allowed 30 attack SMS and refused 16 real logins.

32
00:03:18.417 --> 00:03:19.399
Reading time:

33
00:03:19.679 --> 00:03:21.300
100 min + code.

34
00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:23.503
Part 1.

35
00:03:23.783 --> 00:03:25.703
Introduction and Why It Matters.

36
00:03:26.953 --> 00:03:29.407
A story: the limiter that locked out Jio.

37
00:03:29.687 --> 00:03:40.737
The grocery platform had rate limiting from day one: a Redis token bucket at the gateway (Ch 08), keyed by IP address, 10 OTP requests per hour.

38
00:03:41.017 --> 00:03:42.276
Then, in one month:

39
00:03:42.556 --> 00:03:44.327
Real customers were locked out.

40
00:03:44.607 --> 00:03:49.300
On a Sunday morning, thousands of customers on one mobile carrier couldn't log in.

41
00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:58.583
Carrier-grade NAT puts thousands of phones behind one public IP, and the per-IP OTP limit treated them as one abusive client.

42
00:03:59.933 --> 00:04:03.901
An SMS-pumping attack cost 18 lakh rupees in a weekend.

43
00:04:04.181 --> 00:04:09.962
Fraudsters triggered OTPs to international number ranges where they shared the termination fees.

44
00:04:10.242 --> 00:04:16.407
They used thousands of residential-proxy IPs and never sent more than two OTPs to any number.

45
00:04:16.687 --> 00:04:19.098
No per-IP or per-phone limit fired.

46
00:04:19.378 --> 00:04:22.578
A logistics partner was throttled inside its contract.

47
00:04:22.858 --> 00:04:33.717
Its 1,000 req per second limit was enforced as 125 req per second on each of 8 gateway nodes, and the partner's traffic arrived mostly on one node.

48
00:04:33.997 --> 00:04:38.861
The partner escalated; the account manager doubled the limit to make the complaints stop.

49
00:04:40.211 --> 00:04:41.875
The limiter took down login.

50
00:04:42.155 --> 00:04:44.245
Redis for rate limits had a failover.

51
00:04:44.525 --> 00:04:50.328
The gateway was configured to fail closed, so for 90 seconds nobody could request an OTP.

52
00:04:50.608 --> 00:04:52.421
The SMS vendor throttled us.

53
00:04:52.701 --> 00:04:56.029
The vendor's contract was 500 SMS per second.

54
00:04:56.309 --> 00:05:06.912
During a campaign, notification workers across three services collectively sent 1,200 per second, and the vendor dropped messages, including OTPs.

55
00:05:07.192 --> 00:05:11.864
Each of these is a system design failure of the limiter, not an algorithm choice.

56
00:05:12.144 --> 00:05:19.760
The rebuilt limiter, designed here, starts from what each limit protects, who it limits, where it's enforced, and what happens when it breaks.

57
00:05:21.110 --> 00:05:21.793
Quote:

58
00:05:22.073 --> 00:05:23.737
The thesis of this chapter.

59
00:05:24.017 --> 00:05:39.654
A rate limiter is several different controls sharing one mechanism: abuse prevention (fraud, scraping, credential stuffing), fairness (one customer can't starve others), contracts (partner quotas), and protection of our capacity and of vendors'.

60
00:05:39.934 --> 00:05:52.563
Each needs the right key (L3), the right accuracy and state size (L1), the right enforcement topology for many nodes and regions (L2, L4), and an explicit failure mode (L4).

61
00:05:52.843 --> 00:05:57.238
The algorithm, token bucket, GCRA, sliding window, is the easy part.

62
