192
00:12:34.797 --> 00:12:36.589
356 rupees.

193
00:12:36.869 --> 00:12:38.213
Real users blocked:

194
00:12:38.493 --> 00:12:39.495
3 users.

195
00:12:40.845 --> 00:12:42.851
IP addresses are shared and cheap.

196
00:12:43.131 --> 00:12:48.912
Carrier CGNAT puts hundreds or thousands of real customers behind one IPv4 address.

197
00:12:49.192 --> 00:12:53.139
Residential-proxy networks give attackers thousands of addresses.

198
00:12:53.419 --> 00:12:56.320
Per-IP limits hurt customers more than attackers.

199
00:12:56.600 --> 00:13:01.272
Use them loosely, and per ASN or subnet for clearly abusive networks.

200
00:13:01.552 --> 00:13:07.611
Device fingerprints are cheap to rotate unless backed by attestation (Play Integrity, App Attest).

201
00:13:07.891 --> 00:13:14.973
Phone numbers are the right key against repeated OTPs to one number, but a pump spreads across thousands of numbers.

202
00:13:16.323 --> 00:13:18.499
Key on what the attacker must concentrate.

203
00:13:18.779 --> 00:13:22.769
An SMS pump earns only from certain destination ranges.

204
00:13:23.049 --> 00:13:28.617
Budget those ranges, with limits learned from normal traffic, and the attack has nowhere to hide.

205
00:13:28.897 --> 00:13:35.318
Expect the attacker to adapt: add a per-country and a global international budget, and alert on anomalies.

206
00:13:35.598 --> 00:13:37.006
Spend friction on risk.

207
00:13:37.286 --> 00:13:44.390
Attestation failures and unusual ranges get a challenge, not a block, so the few real users there still get through.

208
00:13:45.740 --> 00:13:52.951
For authenticated APIs, the user id is the natural key: stable, not shared, and costly to multiply.

209
00:13:53.231 --> 00:14:02.148
Limit anonymous endpoints (OTP, search, catalog) with layered keys, and treat the limit as a fraud control with costs on both sides.

