273
00:17:38.245 --> 00:17:41.147
Over the limit (2,000 req per second offered):

274
00:17:41.427 --> 00:17:44.115
Capped; 0.39 calls per request.

275
00:17:45.465 --> 00:17:57.347
Local splits are free but wrong whenever load is uneven, sticky connections, one partner data centre, uneven load-balancing, and nodes come and go with autoscaling, changing N.

276
00:17:57.627 --> 00:18:14.651
Central checks are exact and simple (one atomic Lua script, Ch 18 L3), but add a network round-trip and a store call to every request: fine for thousands of req per second, costly at 450k rule checks per second across AZs.

277
00:18:16.001 --> 00:18:22.316
Token leasing: a node takes a small batch of tokens from the central GCRA and spends them locally.

278
00:18:22.596 --> 00:18:37.529
L2 needed three refinements before leasing matched the central limiter: size leases to each node's recent rate (~50 ms of its traffic), give unused tokens back when a lease expires, and cache denials until tokens are due.

279
00:18:37.809 --> 00:18:43.463
Fixed large leases stranded tokens on quiet nodes and rejected in-contract traffic.

280
00:18:43.743 --> 00:18:50.761
Over the limit, leases shrink because tokens are scarce, so the call rate rises (0.39 per request).

281
00:18:51.041 --> 00:19:04.268
Other options: approximate local limits with periodic count synchronisation (gossip), or a sticky routing of each key to one limiter node (consistent hashing), which moves the hot-key problem to that node.

282
00:19:05.618 --> 00:19:20.381
A practical tiering: local limiters for coarse protection (per-node concurrency, load shedding, Ch 18), leased or central GCRA for customer and partner limits, and central exact checks for low-volume fraud controls.

283
00:19:21.731 --> 00:19:23.160
Multi-region limits.

284
00:19:23.440 --> 00:19:28.432
A global contract served from two regions needs either a global store or a split.

285
