Watch out:

700
00:51:24.137 --> 00:51:24.841
10.

701
00:51:25.121 --> 00:51:26.999
Fail open on fraud controls.

702
00:51:27.279 --> 00:51:28.943
Attackers notice (L4:

703
00:51:29.223 --> 00:51:32.721
9,265 rupeesin 2 minutes).

704
00:51:34.071 --> 00:51:34.989
Watch out:

705
00:51:35.269 --> 00:51:36.101
11.

706
00:51:36.381 --> 00:51:38.727
Enforcing new rules without shadow mode.

707
00:51:39.007 --> 00:51:41.909
False positives land on real customers first.

708
00:51:43.259 --> 00:51:44.176
Watch out:

709
00:51:44.456 --> 00:51:45.245
12.

710
00:51:45.525 --> 00:51:47.061
Rejecting when you should shape.

711
00:51:47.341 --> 00:51:51.864
Outbound vendor limits call for queuing and pacing, with OTP priority.

712
00:51:53.214 --> 00:51:54.089
Part 8.

713
00:51:54.369 --> 00:51:55.713
Interview Q and A.

714
00:51:56.963 --> 00:51:57.795
Beginner.

715
00:51:58.075 --> 00:51:59.099
Question 1.

716
00:51:59.379 --> 00:52:02.429
Design a distributed rate limiter, where do you start?

717
00:52:02.709 --> 00:52:04.075
Thirty-second answer:

718
00:52:04.355 --> 00:52:15.661
With what each limit protects and who it limits: fairness per user, contracts per API key, fraud controls per phone and destination, capacity per service, vendor TPS.

719
00:52:15.941 --> 00:52:19.483
That decides the key, the accuracy needed and the failure mode.

720
00:52:19.763 --> 00:52:33.757
Then the algorithm (GCRA or token bucket by default), where state lives (Redis, local, leased), multi-node and multi-region enforcement, response headers, configuration with shadow mode, and observability.

721
00:52:35.107 --> 00:52:36.110
Question 2.

722
00:52:36.390 --> 00:52:38.673
What is GCRA and why is it popular?

723
00:52:38.953 --> 00:52:40.318
Thirty-second answer:

724
00:52:40.598 --> 00:52:48.491
The Generic Cell Rate Algorithm stores one value per key, the theoretical arrival time of the next request at the allowed rate.

725
00:52:48.771 --> 00:52:56.771
A request is allowed if it isn't earlier than that time minus the burst tolerance; then the time advances by one emission interval.

726
00:52:57.051 --> 00:53:03.153
It behaves exactly like a token bucket but with one integer of state and a natural retry-after.

727
00:53:04.503 --> 00:53:05.548
Question 3.

728
00:53:05.828 --> 00:53:09.348
Why are per-IP limits dangerous for a mobile-first product?

729
00:53:09.628 --> 00:53:10.993
Thirty-second answer:

730
00:53:11.273 --> 00:53:22.217
Carrier-grade NAT puts hundreds or thousands of phones behind one public IPv4 address, so a per-IP limit treats a carrier's customers as one client and locks them out.

731
00:53:22.497 --> 00:53:26.167
Meanwhile attackers rent thousands of residential IPs.

732
00:53:26.447 --> 00:53:30.095
Per-IP limits end up hurting customers more than attackers.

733
00:53:31.445 --> 00:53:32.511
Question 4.

734
00:53:32.791 --> 00:53:35.778
What should a 429 response contain?

735
00:53:36.058 --> 00:53:37.423
Thirty-second answer:

736
00:53:37.703 --> 00:53:49.543
Status 429, a Retry-After header, and rate-limit headers describing the policy and remaining quota (the IETF RateLimit fields) so clients can pace themselves.

737
00:53:49.823 --> 00:53:56.287
For fraud controls, keep the message generic and possibly present a challenge rather than revealing the rule.

738
00:53:57.637 --> 00:53:58.747
Question 5.

739
00:53:59.027 --> 00:54:02.653
Token bucket or sliding window for a 'max 60 per minute' rule?

740
00:54:02.933 --> 00:54:04.299
Thirty-second answer:

741
00:54:04.579 --> 00:54:10.189
If the rule is a rate with some burst, a token bucket or GCRA (60 per minute, burst N).

742
00:54:10.469 --> 00:54:24.891
If it must never exceed 60 in any 60-second span, only an exact sliding log guarantees it; a sliding-window counter is approximate and can exceed the limit under bursty traffic; a fixed window can double it at boundaries.

743
00:54:26.241 --> 00:54:27.243
Intermediate.

744
00:54:27.523 --> 00:54:28.633
Question 6.

745
00:54:28.913 --> 00:54:32.603
How do you enforce one global limit across many gateway nodes?

746
00:54:32.883 --> 00:54:34.249
Thirty-second answer:

747
00:54:34.529 --> 00:54:54.731
Options: split the limit per node (free, wrong under skew and autoscaling); check a central store per request (exact, a round-trip each time); or lease tokens from the central store in small batches sized to each node's rate, giving unused tokens back and caching denials (near-exact with far fewer calls).

748
00:54:55.011 --> 00:54:58.638
Or route each key to one limiter node by consistent hashing.

749
00:54:59.988 --> 00:55:01.076
Question 7.

750
00:55:01.356 --> 00:55:04.385
How much state does a limiter need for 10 million keys?

751
00:55:04.665 --> 00:55:06.031
Thirty-second answer:

752
00:55:06.311 --> 00:55:08.017
Depends on representation:

753
00:55:08.297 --> 00:55:25.556
GCRA ~90 bytes per key in Redis (~0.9 GB for 10M), a token-bucket hash ~123, two window counters ~149, an exact log ~1 KB with 30 entries (~10 GB).

754
00:55:25.836 --> 00:55:31.148
Choose GCRA for most limits and exact logs only for small, short-lived key sets.

755
00:55:32.498 --> 00:55:33.522
Question 8.

756
00:55:33.802 --> 00:55:35.722
How do you roll out a new limit safely?

757
00:55:36.002 --> 00:55:37.367
Thirty-second answer:

758
00:55:37.647 --> 00:55:53.903
Rules as versioned configuration; deploy in shadow mode that logs would-be denials; review false positives against known-good traffic for a week; then enforce gradually (by region or percentage); monitor denials by rule with an easy rollback.

759
00:55:55.253 --> 00:55:56.320
Question 9.

760
00:55:56.600 --> 00:55:59.715
Rate limiting versus load shedding versus backpressure?

761
00:55:59.995 --> 00:56:01.360
Thirty-second answer:

762
00:56:01.640 --> 00:56:08.339
Rate limiting caps what a given client may send (fairness, contracts, abuse), independent of current load.

763
00:56:08.619 --> 00:56:14.080
Load shedding rejects work when the system is overloaded, prioritising important traffic.

764
00:56:14.360 --> 00:56:18.328
Backpressure slows producers by signalling downstream capacity.

765
00:56:18.608 --> 00:56:23.216
You need all three; rate limits alone don't protect against aggregate overload.

766
00:56:24.566 --> 00:56:25.334
Senior.

767
00:56:25.614 --> 00:56:26.659
Question 10.

768
00:56:26.939 --> 00:56:32.550
Design OTP rate limiting that stops SMS pumping without locking out customers.

769
00:56:32.830 --> 00:56:34.195
Thirty-second answer:

770
00:56:34.475 --> 00:56:51.350
Layered keys: per phone (3 or 10 min, 5 or hour, exact), per destination range and country budgets learned from baseline, a global international budget, attested-device checks with a challenge on failure, loose per-ASN limits for known-abusive networks.

771
00:56:51.630 --> 00:56:54.745
Monitor sends versus verified logins per range.

772
00:56:55.025 --> 00:56:59.270
Treat it as a fraud control with a cost on both sides; shadow new rules.

773
00:57:00.620 --> 00:57:01.751
Question 11.

774
00:57:02.031 --> 00:57:03.588
The limiter's Redis is down.

775
00:57:03.868 --> 00:57:04.807
What happens?

776
00:57:05.087 --> 00:57:06.452
Thirty-second answer:

777
00:57:06.732 --> 00:57:08.332
Whatever each rule declares.

778
00:57:08.612 --> 00:57:12.879
Fairness and partner limits fail open (other layers protect capacity).

779
00:57:13.159 --> 00:57:17.447
Fraud controls fall back to local, conservative shares of their budgets.

780
00:57:17.727 --> 00:57:22.463
Fail closed only for narrowly scoped rules where allowing is worse than an outage.

781
00:57:22.743 --> 00:57:27.265
Limiter calls have millisecond timeouts and a breaker; fallback mode alerts.

782
00:57:28.615 --> 00:57:29.703
Question 12.

783
00:57:29.983 --> 00:57:33.973
How do you enforce a partner quota across two active-active regions?

784
00:57:34.253 --> 00:57:35.618
Thirty-second answer:

785
00:57:35.898 --> 00:57:44.154
Give each region a share of the global limit and rebalance shares periodically by recent demand, exchanging counts asynchronously.

786
00:57:44.434 --> 00:57:46.759
Accept a few percent of approximation.

787
00:57:47.039 --> 00:57:55.103
A single central limiter is exact but adds cross-region latency and forces a fail-open or closed choice during partitions.

788
00:57:56.453 --> 00:57:57.733
Question 13.

789
00:57:58.013 --> 00:58:01.640
How do you handle a hot key, one huge partner or a campaign?

790
00:58:01.920 --> 00:58:03.285
Thirty-second answer:

791
00:58:03.565 --> 00:58:07.619
A single key lives on one shard, so its limiter checks concentrate there.

792
00:58:07.899 --> 00:58:14.725
Lease tokens so most requests are decided locally, cache denials, and if needed give the key its own shard.

793
00:58:15.005 --> 00:58:18.888
Shape rather than reject if the traffic is legitimate but bursty.

794
00:58:20.238 --> 00:58:21.433
Question 14.

795
00:58:21.713 --> 00:58:26.001
How do you pace calls to a vendor with a TPS limit, from many services?

796
00:58:26.281 --> 00:58:27.646
Thirty-second answer:

797
00:58:27.926 --> 00:58:41.686
Centralise egress: all services enqueue SMS to one queue; a shaper (leaky bucket) sends at the vendor's TPS minus headroom, with priority classes (OTP before transactional before marketing).

798
00:58:41.966 --> 00:58:47.534
If multiple senders must remain, use a global GCRA with leasing and back-pressure producers.

799
00:58:48.884 --> 00:58:49.780
Architect.

800
00:58:50.060 --> 00:58:51.255
Question 15.

801
00:58:51.535 --> 00:58:55.161
Design the rate-limiting platform for a national grocery app.

802
00:58:55.441 --> 00:58:56.807
Thirty-second answer:

803
00:58:57.087 --> 00:59:10.655
Edge volumetric limits; gateway limits per user and API key with GCRA state in regional Redis and adaptive leasing; service-level exact checks for fraud controls (OTP per phone, range budgets,

804
00:59:10.935 --> 00:59:26.209
attestation); egress shaping for vendors; rules as versioned data with shadow mode; per-rule failure modes with local fallback; cross-region share rebalancing; observability including fraud metrics (sends versus verifications).

805
00:59:27.559 --> 00:59:28.754
Question 16.

806
00:59:29.034 --> 00:59:31.210
How do you measure whether your limits are right?

807
00:59:31.490 --> 00:59:32.855
Thirty-second answer:

808
00:59:33.135 --> 00:59:48.538
Measure both sides: harm prevented (attack spend avoided, abusive traffic dropped, overload incidents avoided) and harm caused (false positives: real users denied, support tickets, partner complaints, conversion drop on denied flows).

809
00:59:48.818 --> 00:59:53.831
Use shadow mode and holdouts for new rules, and review top denied keys regularly.

810
00:59:55.181 --> 00:59:56.525
Question 17.

811
00:59:56.805 --> 00:59:59.835
Build your own limiter or use the gateway's or cloud's?

812
01:00:00.115 --> 01:00:01.480
Thirty-second answer:

813
01:00:01.760 --> 01:00:09.419
Use managed edge or WAF limits for volumetric protection and the gateway's built-in limiter for simple per-key limits.

814
01:00:09.699 --> 01:00:19.235
Build (on shared components) where the business needs it: fraud-aware OTP limits, partner contracts across regions, leasing, per-rule failure modes.

815
01:00:19.515 --> 01:00:21.456
Keep one rule model across both.

816
01:00:22.806 --> 01:00:23.979
Question 18.

817
01:00:24.259 --> 01:00:28.270
How do rate limits interact with multi-tenancy and pricing plans?

818
01:00:28.550 --> 01:00:29.915
Thirty-second answer:

819
01:00:30.195 --> 01:00:38.771
Limits become product features: per-tenant plans with quotas and burst, visible usage, overage policies, and noisy-neighbour protection.

820
01:00:39.051 --> 01:00:51.638
Key by tenant and user, enforce plan limits at the gateway, and keep capacity-protection limits separate from commercial quotas (fail modes differ: quotas may fail open, capacity protection sheds).

821
01:00:52.988 --> 01:00:54.225
Question 19.

822
01:00:54.505 --> 01:00:56.937
An attacker adapts to your OTP limits.

823
01:00:57.217 --> 01:00:58.881
What's your long-term strategy?

824
01:00:59.161 --> 01:01:00.527
Thirty-second answer:

825
01:01:00.807 --> 01:01:19.815
Make attacks expensive and signals rich: attested devices, risk scoring combining many signals (range, device, behaviour, velocity), progressive friction (challenge, delay, voice OTP), per-country budgets, commercial controls with SMS vendors (blocked premium ranges, spend caps),

826
01:01:20.095 --> 01:01:26.409
monitoring send-to-verify ratios, and quick-response tooling for new rules in shadow and enforce modes.

827
01:01:27.759 --> 01:01:28.869
Question 20.

828
01:01:29.149 --> 01:01:32.434
What SLOs and alerts would you set for the limiter itself?

829
01:01:32.714 --> 01:01:34.079
Thirty-second answer:

830
01:01:34.359 --> 01:01:49.613
Added latency p99 under 1 ms at the gateway; limiter store availability; fallback-mode duration; false-positive rate (in-contract rejections for partners, real-user OTP denials); shadow review backlog.

831
01:01:49.893 --> 01:01:59.215
Page on fallback mode and on a sudden rise in denials across many keys (misconfiguration); ticket on shadow-mode hits and drifting baselines.

832
01:02:00.565 --> 01:02:01.461
Part 9.

833
01:02:01.741 --> 01:02:03.149
Summary and Practice.

834
01:02:04.399 --> 01:02:05.487
Key takeaways.

835
01:02:05.767 --> 01:02:12.338
Start from what each limit protects; fairness, contracts, fraud and capacity differ (§1.2).

836
01:02:12.618 --> 01:02:13.898
The key matters most:

837
01:02:14.178 --> 01:02:18.658
IPs are shared and cheap; key on what attackers must concentrate (L3).

838
01:02:18.938 --> 01:02:23.845
GCRA by default: one integer per key, token-bucket semantics (L1).

839
01:02:24.125 --> 01:02:29.693
Sliding counters are approximate; exact logs only where caps must be hard (L1).

840
01:02:29.973 --> 01:02:37.567
Global limits from many nodes: central for exactness, leasing for scale, sized, returned, denial-cached (L2).

841
01:02:38.917 --> 01:02:44.507
Regions: rebalance shares by demand; central limiters struggle with partitions (L4).

842
01:02:44.787 --> 01:02:49.117
Failure mode per rule, with local fallback for fraud controls (L4).

843
01:02:49.397 --> 01:02:51.360
Shadow first, measure both sides.

844
01:02:52.710 --> 01:02:54.033
Decision cheat sheet.

845
01:02:54.313 --> 01:02:55.678
Here is a quick table:

846
01:02:55.958 --> 01:02:57.131
Need, and Choice.

847
01:02:57.411 --> 01:02:59.118
Per-user API fairness:

848
01:02:59.398 --> 01:03:02.833
GCRA per user id at the gateway, leased, fail open.

849
01:03:03.113 --> 01:03:04.350
Partner contract:

850
01:03:04.630 --> 01:03:09.409
GCRA per API key, leasing, cross-region rebalancing, fail open.

851
01:03:09.689 --> 01:03:10.862
OTP abuse:

852
01:03:11.142 --> 01:03:16.070
Exact per phone + range budgets + attestation or challenge, local fallback.

853
01:03:16.350 --> 01:03:17.609
Hard N per window:

854
01:03:17.889 --> 01:03:20.257
Sliding log, low-volume keys only.

855
01:03:20.537 --> 01:03:21.667
Vendor TPS:

856
01:03:21.947 --> 01:03:25.595
Central egress queue + leaky-bucket shaper with priorities.

857
01:03:25.875 --> 01:03:27.198
Volumetric floods:

858
01:03:27.478 --> 01:03:30.294
Edge or WAF per IP or ASN.

859
01:03:31.644 --> 01:03:32.775
Exercises.

860
01:03:33.055 --> 01:03:37.513
(B2) In L1, add a 4th scenario with smoother traffic (no bursts).

861
01:03:37.793 --> 01:03:39.991
How does the sliding counter's error change?

862
01:03:40.271 --> 01:03:45.796
(B3) In L2, add node churn: node 7 leaves at 5 s and a new node joins.

863
01:03:46.076 --> 01:03:47.932
Compare local splits and leasing.

864
01:03:48.212 --> 01:03:52.031
(B3) In L3, make the attacker spread over 50 ranges.

865
01:03:52.311 --> 01:03:56.108
Which additional budget stops it, and what does it cost real users?

866
01:03:56.388 --> 01:04:01.017
(B4) In L4, change the rebalance interval to 1 s and 60 s.

867
01:04:01.297 --> 01:04:04.604
Plot in-contract rejections against exchange traffic.

868
01:04:05.954 --> 01:04:15.234
(B4) Implement a multi-key Lua script that checks phone, range and country budgets atomically and returns the first denial with retry-after.

